# Line endings.
#
# Windows editors traditionally end lines with two characters (CR LF) where
# macOS and Linux use one (LF). Without this file, a Windows learner can clone
# the repo, change nothing, and still see every line of every file marked as
# modified -- which is baffling the first time it happens, and looks like you
# broke something.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` tells Git to store everything with LF and hand your editor
# whatever it needs. You should never have to think about it again.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that are not text at all. Git must not "fix" the line endings inside
# these, because there are no line endings inside these -- only bytes that
# would be corrupted by the attempt.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# Tide observations are real measurements. Keeping them LF means the numbers a
# learner in Hobart commits are byte-identical to the ones read in Halifax.
*.csv text eol=lf
